# PS2 VU Flag Hardware Test

This branch contains a standalone PlayStation 2 hardware test for VU floating-point flag behavior, focused on negative-zero handling in multiply operations.

The test was created while investigating PS2 VU arithmetic accuracy for recompilation/emulation work. Software references disagreed on whether a raw negative-zero result (`0x80000000`) should set the VU Sign flag, so the behavior was measured directly on physical PS2 hardware.

## Hardware

- Retail SCPH-90K-series PlayStation 2 Slim
- Test launched through an existing Fortuna/USB homebrew setup
- No firmware changes
- No persistent installation
- No memory-card writes performed by the test payload

## What is captured

For each test case the payload records:

- Raw arithmetic result
- MAC flags
- STATUS before execution
- STATUS after execution

The test also includes control cases for:

- Exact negative zero
- Underflow-generated negative zero
- Positive zero
- Seeded I/D state
- Component masking
- vf0 behavior
- Overflow
- Ordinary finite arithmetic

## Physical hardware results

Key observed cases:

Case 0 — positive zero times negative value

- Result: `80000000`
- MAC: `00FF`
- STATUS: `0C01` to `0C03`

Case 1 — negative zero times positive value

- Result: `80000000`
- MAC: `00FF`
- STATUS: `0C01` to `0C03`

Case 2 — negative underflow

- Result: `80000000`
- MAC: `0FFF`
- STATUS: `0C01` to `0DC7`

Case 3 — negative zero times negative value

- Result: `00000000`
- MAC: `000F`
- STATUS: `0C01` to `0C41`

Additional test status:

- Harness errors: `0`
- 32/64-bit capture comparison enabled
- Raw RAM capture active
- Result checksum: `17D87C01`

## Important note about emulator results

PCSX2 was used only to sanity-check the test harness, instruction execution, result capture, and display logic.

Emulator arithmetic and flag results are not treated as the hardware oracle.

The values above are from physical PS2 hardware.

## Purpose

This data is intended to help clarify PS2 VU floating-point flag semantics for:

- Emulator accuracy
- Static recompilation
- Dynamic recompilation
- Reverse engineering
- Hardware behavior documentation

The main question being investigated is whether raw negative-zero results set the VU Sign flag, and whether underflow-generated negative zero behaves differently from exact negative zero.

## Reproducibility

The standalone test payload, source, build instructions, and hardware result data should be kept together so the measurements can be reproduced or extended with additional VU cases later.
